/**

@page tutorials_multigrid_matrixfree_jumping_coefficients_and_optimisation Matrix-free DG with jumping coefficients or precomputed cell operators


MGHyPE's DG realisations all support cell-dependent operators, i.e. PDEs where 
the weak form over each cell differs. Probably the simplest equation of that 
type is

@f$
  - \nabla \left( \epsilon(x) \nabla \right) u = f
@f$ 

with a space-dependent material parameter @f$ \epsilon @f$. The environments' 
vanilla configuration is prepared for such setups, even though, by default, it 
uses an invariant cell operator, i.e. the same operator per cell. The other way 
round, the code does not exploit any knowledge about invariant operators 
either, which means that we ignore some very low-hanging fruits regarding
runtime optimisations.

<img src="https://www.spe.org/web/csp/images/img1.gif" />

(image from the SPE10 benchmark with jumping coefficients; original URL: https://www.spe.org/web/csp/datasets/set02.htm)


## Code architecture

Each run of a DG solver generates two classes: An abstract solver class and the
particular implementation. The abstract base class is regenerated every time 
you rerun the Python script, as it mirrors all the entries from the script such
as polynomial orders or the matrices from the Python class' constructor. The 
particular subclass is meant to be the place where you tailor the PDE towards
your needs for all features that are not available directly through the Python
script.

 
@image html jumping-coefficients-and-optimisation.png


By default, the base class befills all interesting assembly routines with the 
matrices from the Python script, and the only thing the user has to write is
the function

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
void MySolver::initCellDoF(
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  x,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  h,
  tarch::la::Vector< UnknownsPerCellNode, double >&    value,
  tarch::la::Vector< UnknownsPerCellNode, double >&    rhs,
  tarch::la::Vector< Dimensions, int >&         node
) {
  logTraceInWith4Arguments("initCellDoF(...)", x, h, value, rhs);

  rhs[0]   = // something fancy
  value[0] = 0;

  logTraceOutWith4Arguments("initCellDoF(...)", x, h, value, rhs);
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The two routines that are relevant for the discussion in this chapter
are however those accessing the assembly and approximate system matrices.

By default, they work with the matrices passed in through the Python 
script. If the argument there is None, the generated superclass routine
will be abstract, and you will have to implement these two routines 
yourself. If you hand in some matrix, you still can overwrite and 
redefine these routines yourself later on.


## Jumping coefficients

Yet to be written.


## Precomputed cell operators

By default, MGHyPE takes the cell operator and inverts this one per cell with 
a LU decomposition. LU might be very slow. If the operator in the cell is known
beforehand and is the same in all cells, the whole LU business generates a lot
of MFLOPS without any sense: It is way better to invert the cell operator once,
to memorise this outcome and then to reuse it.


### Cell update operator

To do so, we open the header of MySolver and declare that we will overwrite the
cell operator:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    virtual tarch::la::Matrix< CellUnknowns, CellUnknowns, double > getInvertedApproxSystemMatrix(
      const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellCentre,
      const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellSize
    ) override;
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Before we continue, we quickly check that the autogenerated application routine 
in abstract base class uses this getter too:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tarch::la::Vector< ... > AbstractMySolver::applyInvertedApproxSystemMatrix(
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&     cellCentre,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&     cellSize,
  const tarch::la::Vector< CellUnknowns, double >& v
) {
  return getInvertedApproxSystemMatrix(cellCentre,cellSize) * v;
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    
Hence, we only need to provide a faster implementation of 
getInvertedApproxSystemMatrix() and should get significantly faster 
performance. Obviously, we can also manually tune 
applyInvertedApproxSystemMatrix(), but that's for the future. 

The simplest way to optimise getInvertedApproxSystemMatrix() is to cache the LU
decomposition's outcome. In the example below, I assume that the we know the
system matrix, but that it lacks a proper scaling with a material parameter:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
#include "tarch/la/LUDecomposition.h"


tarch::la::Matrix< ... > MySolver::getInvertedApproxSystemMatrix(
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellCentre,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellSize
) {
  static tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>  unitCellCentre(0.0);
  static tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>  unitCellSize(1.0);

  static tarch::la::Matrix< CellUnknowns, CellUnknowns, double > resultOnReferenceCell = AbstractDGPoissonPrecompute::getInvertedApproxSystemMatrix(
    unitCellCentre,
    unitCellSize
  );

  #if Dimensions==2
  return resultOnReferenceCell;
  #else
  return 1.0/cellSize(0) * resultOnReferenceCell;
  #endif
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In the example above, we use the base class' inverted operator on the unit 
cube. This is for a scaled Poisson operator, so we can use this one directly
as long as we work in two dimensions. In three dimensions, we have to rescale.

This is obvious one of the simplest variants one can think of, but it 
illustrates the fundamental idea of a very simple opimisation: We intert the 
operator per cell only once and then apply this one from hereon.

### Cell assembly

We can now do exactly the same "trick" for the cell operator, too. We tell
MGHyPE that we will overwrite its built-in assembly

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    virtual tarch::la::Matrix< ... > getLocalAssemblyMatrix(
      const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellCentre,
      const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellSize
    ) override;

    virtual tarch::la::Vector< CellUnknowns, double > applyLocalAssemblyMatrix(
      const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&      cellCentre,
      const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&      cellSize,
      const tarch::la::Vector< CellUnknowns, double >&  v
    ) override;
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and then we implement them in our user class:


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tarch::la::Matrix< ... > MySolver::getLocalAssemblyMatrix(
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellCentre,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&  cellSize
) {
  static tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>  unitCellCentre(0.0);
  static tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>  unitCellSize(1.0);

  static tarch::la::Matrix< CellUnknowns, CellUnknowns, double > resultOnReferenceCell = AbstractMySolver::getLocalAssemblyMatrix(
    unitCellCentre,
    unitCellSize
  );

  #if Dimensions==2
  return resultOnReferenceCell;
  #else
  return cellSize(0) * resultOnReferenceCell;
 #endif
}


tarch::la::Vector< ... > MySolver::applyLocalAssemblyMatrix(
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&      cellCentre,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&      cellSize,
  const tarch::la::Vector< CellUnknowns, double >&  v
) {
  return getLocalAssemblyMatrix(cellCentre,cellSize) * v;
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The abstract version (study the generated code in there) has an alternative 
implementation of applyLocalAssemblyMatrix(), where we use the helper routine
::mghype::applyMatrixFromHWeightedLinearCombination() which accumulates the 
outcome in-situ. If we can precompute the operator, we can directly call 
the mat-vec and rely on the compiler to make this thing fast, hoping that the 
compiler recognises that the matrix is static. Alternatively, we can also 
cache the outcome in  applyLocalAssemblyMatrix() via an additional static 
variable.
In this case, we obtain


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tarch::la::Vector< ... > MySolver::applyLocalAssemblyMatrix(
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&      cellCentre,
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>&      cellSize,
  const tarch::la::Vector< CellUnknowns, double >&  v
) {
  static tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>  unitCellCentre(0.0);
  static tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>  unitCellSize(1.0);

  static tarch::la::Matrix< CellUnknowns, CellUnknowns, double > resultOnReferenceCell = AbstractMySolver::getLocalAssemblyMatrix(
    unitCellCentre,
    unitCellSize
  );

  #if Dimensions==2
  return resultOnReferenceCell * v;
  #else
  return cellSize(0) * resultOnReferenceCell * v;
  #endif
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


If you have a more complicated operator, where you can pre-assemble parts of 
the matrices, you effectively end up with a hybrid of this method and the 
vanilla one. 
All of our tweaks here rely on the fact that the abstract base class has the
operator matrices available, so we can build our code using them. 
If you pass in None into the Python script, the base class routines will be 
abstract, i.e. you cannot use them in your bespoke code and you have to write
all operators yourself.

*/
